<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pentecost</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Whit Sunday, Whitsunday, Whitsun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pentecost is a Christian holiday held on the 49th day after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> (the 50th by inclusive counting), commemorating the descent of the Holy Spirit upon the Apostles and other followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> during the Feast of Weeks, as described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a>. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a> pentēkostē, fiftieth, a word the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a> used for Shavuot, the Jewish harvest festival held on the fiftieth day after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a>. It is called the birthday of the Church. As its date depends on Easter it is a moveable feast; it is one of the Great feasts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a> and a Solemnity of the Catholic Church, and the Monday after it is a legal holiday in many countries.</p></div>
